try it, I have a demo available, so come to

01:18:54.422 --> 01:18:55.082
me after this talk.

01:18:57.482 --> 01:18:59.932
So So now we have high resolution

01:19:00.152 --> 01:19:02.242
real time models that can be trained in

01:19:02.242 --> 01:19:04.522
about hundred hours of gameplay, which is

01:19:05.162 --> 01:19:07.192
pretty good progress from our original one.

01:19:07.442 --> 01:19:08.467
We're still

01:19:09.452 --> 01:19:11.531
have the main issue of we have a fixed set

01:19:11.532 --> 01:19:13.762
of gameplay mechanics that we cannot deviate from.

01:19:14.081 --> 01:19:16.322
So how can we try new ideas quickly

01:19:16.322 --> 01:19:18.472
and prototype them in the world

01:19:18.472 --> 01:19:20.072
model? Without degrading its quality?

01:19:21.752 --> 01:19:24.032
So the common idea thinking

01:19:24.332 --> 01:19:26.362
of generating

01:19:26.502 --> 01:19:28.662
something new is immediately scaling up to

01:19:28.662 --> 01:19:30.022
more diverse content.

01:19:30.902 --> 01:19:33.061
The problem with scaling up with very diverse

01:19:33.062 --> 01:19:34.722
data is that behaviors

01:19:35.182 --> 01:19:37.402
that emerge are not necessarily

01:19:37.402 --> 01:19:39.701
predictable and not necessarily easy to

01:19:39.702 --> 01:19:41.722
trigger reliably. So we decided

01:19:41.722 --> 01:19:43.762
to look at a different approach, which is

01:19:43.762 --> 01:19:45.932
more focused on control to make sure

01:19:45.932 --> 01:19:48.021
that anything any behavior that

01:19:48.021 --> 01:19:50.212
we generate have as can be

01:19:50.212 --> 01:19:51.962
triggered very predictably and reliably.

01:19:52.682 --> 01:19:54.942
To achieve this, we looked at building targeted

01:19:55.242 --> 01:19:57.081
small sets dataset,

01:19:57.722 --> 01:19:59.782
small scale datasets using parameter

01:19:59.782 --> 01:20:02.022
efficient fine tuning to avoid degrading the quality

01:20:02.022 --> 01:20:04.202
of the original model and using

01:20:04.582 --> 01:20:06.742
an explicit trigger mechanism to make these new behaviors

01:20:06.742 --> 01:20:08.642
happen. In this case, we use text.

01:20:10.482 --> 01:20:12.021
As a proof of concept,

01:20:12.742 --> 01:20:14.811
for environment editing, we use a

01:20:14.811 --> 01:20:16.912
task which we call body swap. Where the

01:20:16.912 --> 01:20:19.232
objective is to swap the playable character

01:20:19.232 --> 01:20:20.932
to another using a test instruction.

01:20:21.352 --> 01:20:23.432
So this is not a very complex task and we

01:20:23.432 --> 01:20:25.052
use this for a few reasons.

01:20:26.342 --> 01:20:28.362
Firstly, this is a task that is very easy

01:20:28.982 --> 01:20:30.552
to evaluate both qualitatively and quantitatively.

01:20:31.132 --> 01:20:33.372
So for quantitative evaluation, we fine

01:20:33.372 --> 01:20:35.552
tune the VLM to recognize the characters.

01:20:36.362 --> 01:20:38.532
Then we can prompt the model. To verify that

01:20:38.532 --> 01:20:40.552
the swap has happened. This is also

01:20:40.552 --> 01:20:42.712
a task that we can simulate pretty easily in

01:20:42.712 --> 01:20:43.712
our training dataset.

01:20:45.072 --> 01:20:46.692
And to do this, we curated

01:20:48.622 --> 01:20:50.792
data sets to 55,000 training

01:20:50.792 --> 01:20:52.952
samples, which is very, very small compared to

01:20:52.952 --> 01:20:54.872
our original seven years of gameplay.

01:20:55.752 --> 01:20:57.832
And the idea was to match different sequences

01:20:57.832 --> 01:20:59.241
in our warm training set

01:20:59.882 --> 01:21:02.272
finding locations on the map where

01:21:02.492 --> 01:21:04.942
the characters are on the same location, similar camera

01:21:05.162 --> 01:21:06.962
angle, and doing similar actions.

01:21:10.072 --> 01:21:12.321
To enable fine tuning our model

01:21:12.562 --> 01:21:14.182
with the body swap datasets,

01:21:14.642 --> 01:21:16.582
was actually pretty easy because of the architecture

01:21:16.992 --> 01:21:19.152
the transformer based architecture. We could just

01:21:19.152 --> 01:21:21.462
integrate the text our sequence.

01:21:21.462 --> 01:21:23.232
And learn the model to swap. Based

01:21:23.872 --> 01:21:26.081
on this text. The text prompt that we use

01:21:26.081 --> 01:21:28.422
are based on character appearance, character

01:21:29.262 --> 01:21:31.442
skills in the game, and also the character's

01:21:31.502 --> 01:21:31.842
name.

01:21:33.842 --> 01:21:36.002
So this is what a VolleySwap model

01:21:36.002 --> 01:21:38.092
looks like. We tested three

01:21:38.092 --> 01:21:39.692
different ways of fine tuning the model.

01:21:40.172 --> 01:21:42.352
Full fine tuning, which does degrade the quality

01:21:42.412 --> 01:21:44.692
of the world model originally. And two

01:21:44.692 --> 01:21:46.852
parameter efficient fine tuning methods, LoRa and

01:21:46.852 --> 01:21:49.091
DORA. We didn't go very much in-depth

01:21:49.652 --> 01:21:51.632
into using the best possible methods.

01:21:51.792 --> 01:21:54.032
But the key idea here was to gather insights

01:21:54.032 --> 01:21:56.112
on how we could find your models on these small

01:21:56.112 --> 01:21:57.712
datasets. So

01:21:58.222 --> 01:21:59.802
some of the key learnings here

01:22:01.332 --> 01:22:03.492
fully functioning the model leads to the

01:22:03.492 --> 01:22:05.672
best performance in terms of swapping characters.

01:22:05.672 --> 01:22:07.692
This happens very reliably pretty much

01:22:07.692 --> 01:22:09.802
with any prompt that you provide. However,

01:22:09.802 --> 01:22:11.952
the quality of the world will degrades,

01:22:12.232 --> 01:22:14.312
which is why the quantitative score of the

01:22:14.312 --> 01:22:16.572
unique prompts a

01:22:16.572 --> 01:22:17.652
bit lower than you would expect.

01:22:18.772 --> 01:22:20.822
Because the VLM has

01:22:20.822 --> 01:22:23.322
been trained on real world on real data,

01:22:24.392 --> 01:22:26.472
and might struggle a bit more to recognize

01:22:26.472 --> 01:22:28.502
the characters. The

01:22:28.641 --> 01:22:30.721
LoRa and Dora models are doing really well when

01:22:30.722 --> 01:22:32.902
the prompts are very specific. So, for example,

01:22:33.142 --> 01:22:35.002
swap me to the character called buttercup.

01:22:35.562 --> 01:22:38.062
But are struggling a bit more when the prompts are ambiguous

01:22:38.202 --> 01:22:40.286
and there's multiple potential characters

01:22:40.286 --> 01:22:42.461
it could swap to. So for example, swap

01:22:42.461 --> 01:22:43.872
me to a character who's a female.

01:22:45.872 --> 01:22:47.912
So the main takeaway here is that we are able to

01:22:47.912 --> 01:22:50.272
learn these new mechanisms trigger them,

01:22:51.512 --> 01:22:53.532
very reliably There's still a bit

01:22:53.532 --> 01:22:55.612
of work to find the best fine tuning

01:22:55.612 --> 01:22:57.722
methods, to

01:22:57.722 --> 01:22:59.992
find the best way to both find a balance

01:22:59.992 --> 01:23:02.222
between entirely preserving the the world model.

01:23:02.222 --> 01:23:02.722
And,

01:23:04.282 --> 01:23:06.382
achieving good quality new mechanisms.

01:23:07.982 --> 01:23:10.061
So summarizing everything that's happened so

01:23:10.062 --> 01:23:10.502
far,

01:23:12.392 --> 01:23:14.792
we have one. Single model

01:23:15.112 --> 01:23:16.802
has demonstrated that it can learn

01:23:17.282 --> 01:23:19.712
game physics, character behaviors, environment in

01:23:20.382 --> 01:23:22.072
dynamics from role gameplay video.

