Now does the model know the learn of physics?

01:23:25.751 --> 01:23:27.831
Definitely not. It but it does

01:23:27.831 --> 01:23:30.012
learn how to transition from one state to the next

01:23:30.652 --> 01:23:32.731
in a way that is consistent with the physics

01:23:32.732 --> 01:23:34.781
of the game. We then have

01:23:34.782 --> 01:23:37.232
one more t, our model which is improvement

01:23:37.372 --> 01:23:39.182
of a one achieving real time inference

01:23:39.501 --> 01:23:41.581
allowing to play the model as you would play a game.

01:23:43.062 --> 01:23:44.682
Building one more T, we've also managed

01:23:45.141 --> 01:23:47.152
to achieve training models with

01:23:47.152 --> 01:23:49.262
substantially less data. Going from seven years to

01:23:49.262 --> 01:23:51.482
hundred hours of gameplay. And evidence

01:23:51.782 --> 01:23:53.951
that the model generalizes The approach

01:23:53.952 --> 01:23:55.732
generalizes across multiple games.

01:23:56.742 --> 01:23:58.982
We have, early stage one two

01:23:58.982 --> 01:24:01.132
point o and g resolution model

01:24:01.132 --> 01:24:02.912
at real time inference. Again,

01:24:03.391 --> 01:24:04.992
trying on a hundred hours of gameplay.

01:24:05.871 --> 01:24:08.212
And we have experiments trying out how we can

01:24:08.612 --> 01:24:10.691
learn new controllable behaviors, learn

01:24:10.692 --> 01:24:12.882
new game mechanics with a very small datasets

01:24:12.882 --> 01:24:15.142
and very targeted experiments. So

01:24:15.142 --> 01:24:17.331
obviously, our body swap is a proof of concept, and there's

01:24:17.331 --> 01:24:19.561
a lot more that we could try. We are quite interested

01:24:19.562 --> 01:24:22.041
in seeing how we cure can curate more

01:24:22.262 --> 01:24:23.172
complexity mechanisms with

01:24:24.882 --> 01:24:25.862
