02:00:51.462 --> 02:00:53.622
that additional experience to make your model even

02:00:53.622 --> 02:00:55.741
better. Use that better model to make controller

02:00:55.742 --> 02:00:57.762
even better, and then repeat forever.

02:00:58.141 --> 02:01:00.222
So that's a a basic RL loop and pretty

02:01:00.222 --> 02:01:02.411
much any RL algorithm whether model based or model

02:01:02.411 --> 02:01:04.751
free is gonna have some building blocks like this.

02:01:05.392 --> 02:01:07.472
And improving the policy actually

02:01:07.472 --> 02:01:09.491
has a very simple mathematical definition.

02:01:09.492 --> 02:01:11.731
You just want to make the value of that policy,

02:01:11.732 --> 02:01:14.172
value is defined as the expected cumulative

02:01:14.172 --> 02:01:15.952
sum of rewards. You wanna make that value

02:01:16.271 --> 02:01:18.402
high as possible in the initial state. What improve the

02:01:18.402 --> 02:01:20.712
policy means. And what's the purpose

02:01:20.712 --> 02:01:22.542
of the model? To estimate v.

02:01:23.422 --> 02:01:25.561
The model tells you how good your policy is.

02:01:25.642 --> 02:01:27.962
The model does a good job of telling you how good your policy

02:01:27.962 --> 02:01:29.962
is, and allows you to, of course, evaluate

02:01:30.202 --> 02:01:32.282
counterfactual decisions, then you can use it to

02:01:32.282 --> 02:01:34.442
make your policy better. So a

02:01:34.442 --> 02:01:36.782
model needs to be able to evaluate the policy so that

02:01:36.842 --> 02:01:38.932
it can improve In fact, that is the only thing it needs

02:01:38.932 --> 02:01:40.941
to be able to do. There's a lot riding

02:01:40.942 --> 02:01:43.021
on the word evaluate here. It's not a

02:01:43.022 --> 02:01:44.882
simple concept, but if you can do it,

02:01:45.042 --> 02:01:47.082
you can make your policy better, and that is all you need.

02:01:47.962 --> 02:01:50.041
So starting from this basic starting point,

02:01:50.042 --> 02:01:51.942
there's this old idea that is,

02:01:52.422 --> 02:01:54.621
quite, neat, I think, called

02:01:54.622 --> 02:01:57.122
a successor representation. And the successor representation

02:01:57.422 --> 02:01:59.532
is a mathematical object that

02:01:59.592 --> 02:02:01.752
blurs the line between world models and

02:02:01.752 --> 02:02:03.922
value functions. And here's how it works.

02:02:03.922 --> 02:02:06.102
This slide is gonna have a bit of math on it, but

02:02:06.402 --> 02:02:08.661
I'll try to keep this, you know, fairly simple.

02:02:08.661 --> 02:02:09.972
This is not a very mathy talk.

02:02:10.982 --> 02:02:13.141
So, if you write down the value function,

02:02:13.141 --> 02:02:15.412
this is like literally a text value

02:02:15.632 --> 02:02:17.712
function, it's the sum over all the time steps

02:02:17.712 --> 02:02:18.762
from now until the end,

02:02:20.121 --> 02:02:22.592
of the discounted expected reward. Expected under what? Well, expected

02:02:22.592 --> 02:02:24.891
under the system dynamics, the transition function,

02:02:25.052 --> 02:02:27.272
and your policy. Okay? That all makes sense.

02:02:27.492 --> 02:02:29.541
This is typically what a world model would predict.

02:02:29.542 --> 02:02:31.082
It would predict your system dynamics.

02:02:32.212 --> 02:02:34.292
But now, we're going to rearrange these terms a little

02:02:34.292 --> 02:02:36.372
bit and find out that we actually don't need

02:02:36.372 --> 02:02:38.612
a model that predicts the next state.

02:02:38.772 --> 02:02:40.842
We actually get away with something more

02:02:41.452 --> 02:02:42.962
abstract. So,

02:02:43.502 --> 02:02:45.842
what we'll do next is we'll write this

02:02:46.391 --> 02:02:48.652
in kind of a funny way, as a sum

02:02:48.712 --> 02:02:50.342
over all of the future time

02:02:50.762 --> 02:02:52.801
steps where we're taking the

02:02:52.802 --> 02:02:55.041
expectation under the probability of seeing

02:02:55.041 --> 02:02:57.192
a state s t prime at time step

02:02:57.192 --> 02:02:59.302
t prime in the future, given that we're currently

02:02:59.302 --> 02:03:01.332
in state s t. It's like, if I'm currently

02:03:01.332 --> 02:03:03.572
here at time one, what is my distribution of

02:03:03.572 --> 02:03:05.472
states at time 100? Okay.

02:03:05.712 --> 02:03:07.902
Ordinarily, you would do this by marginalizing

02:03:07.962 --> 02:03:10.211
out the distribution overall at intermediate states,

02:03:10.372 --> 02:03:12.532
but let's not worry about that for now. And

02:03:12.532 --> 02:03:14.762
then, I'll expand out the expectation as a

02:03:14.922 --> 02:03:16.702
sum. That's just by definition of expectation.

02:03:17.081 --> 02:03:19.072
And then I'll rearrange the the

02:03:19.422 --> 02:03:21.702
summation terms so that I group all the probabilities

02:03:21.762 --> 02:03:24.042
together. So notice that

02:03:24.332 --> 02:03:26.352
I'm multiplying everything by the reward of some state,

02:03:26.352 --> 02:03:28.431
so I can take the state outside of the summation

02:03:28.432 --> 02:03:30.922
over time steps, and now it's a sum over all

02:03:30.982 --> 02:03:33.001
states. Inside of it I have a sum over all

02:03:33.002 --> 02:03:35.082
the time steps, the probability of landing in

02:03:35.082 --> 02:03:37.312
each state at each time step. Multiply

02:03:37.312 --> 02:03:39.392
by the discount, and around that I multiply by the

02:03:39.392 --> 02:03:41.501
reward. So notice that all the probabilities

02:03:41.501 --> 02:03:43.521
are grouped around these inside these parentheses.

02:03:44.212 --> 02:03:46.712
And I can take this thing around inside these parentheses

02:03:47.052 --> 02:03:49.202
and I can treat that

02:03:49.202 --> 02:03:51.592
as a single monolithic distribution over

02:03:51.592 --> 02:03:53.662
states. And specifically, it's

02:03:53.662 --> 02:03:54.781
a distribution that,

02:03:55.792 --> 02:03:57.871
has a geometric distribution over

02:03:57.872 --> 02:03:59.942
time steps and inside of it, the

02:03:59.942 --> 02:04:02.102
probability of reaching a particular state at

02:04:02.102 --> 02:04:04.112
that time step. And this thing

02:04:04.112 --> 02:04:06.212
is called, the successor representation.

02:04:06.272 --> 02:04:08.312
It was invented by Peter Diane in

02:04:08.312 --> 02:04:10.252
1993. And

02:04:11.052 --> 02:04:12.992
essentially, what this distribution represents

02:04:13.132 --> 02:04:15.292
is how likely am I am I to

02:04:15.292 --> 02:04:17.452
see a particular state let's call it

02:04:17.452 --> 02:04:19.752
s future, if I start in state

02:04:19.752 --> 02:04:22.031
s t? And that kind of makes

02:04:22.032 --> 02:04:24.092
sense. Like, if all you care about is the total

02:04:24.092 --> 02:04:26.212
reward you're gonna get, you don't actually

02:04:26.212 --> 02:04:28.202
care about when you're gonna get that reward.

02:04:28.362 --> 02:04:30.397
All you care about is that at some point

02:04:30.397 --> 02:04:32.461
in the future, some I don't care which

02:04:32.462 --> 02:04:34.542
point, at some point in the future, I am likely to see

02:04:34.542 --> 02:04:36.802
this state. And this is

02:04:36.802 --> 02:04:38.862
simpler than a full

02:04:39.102 --> 02:04:41.182
world model in the sense that it doesn't actually require

02:04:41.182 --> 02:04:43.562
you to think about the sequence in which these things will happen.

02:04:43.722 --> 02:04:45.802
Just care about how likely this to happen at some point

02:04:45.802 --> 02:04:47.882
in the future. If I buy this ingredient, how

02:04:47.882 --> 02:04:49.962
likely am I to eventually end up with a tasty salad in

02:04:49.962 --> 02:04:52.312
front of me? I don't care if it's an hour from now

02:04:52.452 --> 02:04:54.532
or two hours from now. What I care about

02:04:54.532 --> 02:04:55.902
is how tasty the salad is.

02:04:57.262 --> 02:04:59.342
So this kind of quote unquote model is

02:04:59.342 --> 02:05:01.452
actually sufficient you want to evaluate and

02:05:01.452 --> 02:05:03.902
improve policies because it's

02:05:03.902 --> 02:05:06.182
literally derived from the equation for a value function.

02:05:06.501 --> 02:05:08.512
So this derivation basically shows you that

02:05:08.512 --> 02:05:10.671
this model is all you need if you wanna know how good your

02:05:10.672 --> 02:05:12.842
policy is. And it's

02:05:12.842 --> 02:05:14.692
simpler than a traditional world model

02:05:14.852 --> 02:05:16.932
because it doesn't care about when something will happen,

02:05:16.932 --> 02:05:19.082
it just cares about the probability

02:05:19.082 --> 02:05:21.111
that some kind of state will be reached at

02:05:21.112 --> 02:05:23.537
some point in the future. So,

02:05:24.262 --> 02:05:26.421
So, essentially, it's saying, I know this will happen

02:05:26.422 --> 02:05:28.581
in the future if I take this if

02:05:28.582 --> 02:05:30.902
I start in the state, or if you want the queue version

02:05:30.902 --> 02:05:32.732
of this if I take this action in this state.

02:05:33.212 --> 02:05:35.292
So I think that's actually pretty interesting. And I think

02:05:35.292 --> 02:05:37.642
in some ways, this type of model is actually

02:05:37.642 --> 02:05:38.802
more intuitive

02:05:39.792 --> 02:05:42.112
to me as a as a person because I don't typically

02:05:42.112 --> 02:05:44.362
think of things in terms of the exact

02:05:44.362 --> 02:05:46.592
occurrence of those events in time. If I want

02:05:46.592 --> 02:05:48.532
to go to the airport, to fly,

02:05:48.772 --> 02:05:50.931
back to California, I'm not thinking like,

02:05:50.932 --> 02:05:53.206
hey, I need to know, leave this

02:05:53.322 --> 02:05:55.524
building exactly you know, thirty seven seconds

02:05:55.525 --> 02:05:57.512
from now, get into the the car,

02:05:57.672 --> 02:06:00.012
you know, two minutes after that, reach the airport,

02:06:00.432 --> 02:06:02.522
thirty two minutes and fifteen seconds later,

02:06:02.682 --> 02:06:04.622
just think about the probability of those events

02:06:04.762 --> 02:06:06.812
happening success successfully at some

02:06:06.812 --> 02:06:07.962
term the near future.

02:06:10.282 --> 02:06:11.822
Now, a few technical details.

02:06:12.851 --> 02:06:15.272
The cool thing about these kinds of successor

02:06:15.331 --> 02:06:17.472
representations that they can actually be represented as

02:06:17.472 --> 02:06:19.541
value functions. So you can take this

02:06:19.542 --> 02:06:21.702
probability of reaching some state s future given

02:06:21.702 --> 02:06:23.752
that you start in s t, and and, you

02:06:23.752 --> 02:06:26.122
can represent it as value function

02:06:26.642 --> 02:06:28.721
that takes in two arguments, the current state

02:06:28.722 --> 02:06:30.882
and the future state. Let's call it s plus just to make

02:06:30.882 --> 02:06:32.382
the notation a little bit more concise.

02:06:32.942 --> 02:06:34.962
And you can actually express this thing

02:06:35.442 --> 02:06:37.522
via a recursive relationship very similar to

02:06:37.522 --> 02:06:39.592
the Bellman equation. It

02:06:39.632 --> 02:06:41.762
has two terms. The

02:06:41.972 --> 02:06:44.202
current reward and

02:06:44.202 --> 02:06:46.282
the future value. And it's just

02:06:46.282 --> 02:06:48.401
like a bellman equation

02:06:48.402 --> 02:06:50.481
where the reward is an indicator

