02:18:08.142 --> 02:18:10.222
for intentions? Well, a good representation for intentions is the

02:18:10.222 --> 02:18:12.332
reward you wanna maximize. If you intend to make a

02:18:12.332 --> 02:18:14.712
tasty salad, then you plug

02:18:14.712 --> 02:18:15.281
that in for z.

02:18:16.736 --> 02:18:19.102
So how do we do this? Well, remember

02:18:19.642 --> 02:18:21.652
that values are formed by

02:18:21.652 --> 02:18:24.112
summing over your successor representation

02:18:24.172 --> 02:18:26.342
times the reward. Now, doing

02:18:26.342 --> 02:18:28.201
this over all states in in big continuous

02:18:28.502 --> 02:18:30.711
state spaces is intractable because you have infinitely

02:18:30.711 --> 02:18:32.893
many states, we're gonna do it in a feature

02:18:32.893 --> 02:18:34.932
space. And the particular feature space that is

02:18:34.932 --> 02:18:37.011
most natural to use here is actually

02:18:37.012 --> 02:18:39.141
the same feature space we use to represent

02:18:39.142 --> 02:18:41.292
s plus. Why? Because

02:18:41.292 --> 02:18:43.530
the feature space we're using to represent s plus, that's

02:18:43.531 --> 02:18:45.682
the thing that we're predicting over So if we can

02:18:45.682 --> 02:18:47.622
embed the rewards into that same space,

02:18:47.952 --> 02:18:50.122
then that's the natural representation to use

02:18:50.122 --> 02:18:52.302
one of these intention condition value functions.

02:18:52.462 --> 02:18:54.471
So we'll represent z's literally as

02:18:55.512 --> 02:18:57.902
linear combinations of featurized future states

02:18:58.462 --> 02:19:00.762
and rewards. So, basically, the way you can compute this

02:19:00.762 --> 02:19:02.831
thing is sample a bunch of states that

02:19:02.831 --> 02:19:04.932
you like, that you have rewards for,

02:19:05.332 --> 02:19:07.552
featurize them, and average them together weighted

02:19:07.552 --> 02:19:09.192
by their reward. And that gives you a c.

02:19:10.232 --> 02:19:12.391
And then if you want to figure out how

02:19:12.392 --> 02:19:14.807
good your intention is

02:19:15.232 --> 02:19:17.312
for, like, achieving what it's supposed to do, then

02:19:17.312 --> 02:19:19.472
you basically just plug it in for both the second and third

02:19:19.472 --> 02:19:21.882
arguments. So you say, given this state, how

02:19:21.942 --> 02:19:24.022
likely am I to have a tasty salad if I'm

02:19:24.022 --> 02:19:26.142
intending to have a tasty salad? And then you

02:19:26.142 --> 02:19:28.382
just plug you pick that as a z because that's what you wanna

02:19:28.382 --> 02:19:30.502
accomplish. You can also evaluate counterfactors if you

02:19:30.502 --> 02:19:32.652
want. And that might be useful if you

02:19:32.652 --> 02:19:34.842
have if you believe that you haven't fully

02:19:34.843 --> 02:19:37.343
trained this thing successfully for every possible intention,

02:19:37.552 --> 02:19:39.712
and there are ways of doing that as well, but this is the simplest

02:19:39.712 --> 02:19:41.831
way to do it. Okay? So train

02:19:41.831 --> 02:19:44.082
on randomly selected start states, end states,

02:19:44.082 --> 02:19:46.401
and random intentions, train it for all

02:19:46.401 --> 02:19:48.512
these combinations. Okay. And

02:19:48.513 --> 02:19:50.852
I should say this concept borrow borrows very heavily

02:19:50.872 --> 02:19:53.182
a very nice paper introduced something called

02:19:53.182 --> 02:19:55.382
the FB representation, by Tuarete

02:19:55.382 --> 02:19:57.092
and Olivier. So

02:19:57.432 --> 02:19:59.512
Okay. So there are a couple of ways

02:19:59.512 --> 02:20:01.602
you could use this. One way you could use this

02:20:01.602 --> 02:20:03.892
is for zero shot policy recovery.

02:20:03.892 --> 02:20:05.972
This is kind of the analog of planning.

02:20:06.212 --> 02:20:08.427
So, basically, once you've trained one of these,

02:20:09.037 --> 02:20:10.852
things, then just compute the z

02:20:11.092 --> 02:20:13.252
corresponds to your reward function. So you need a few

02:20:13.252 --> 02:20:15.412
states that are labeled with rewards, average

02:20:15.412 --> 02:20:17.483
them together, weight the features

02:20:17.483 --> 02:20:19.643
of the future states by the reward, and just plug

02:20:19.643 --> 02:20:21.472
in that z, so that's

02:20:21.712 --> 02:20:23.832
the analog of planning. You can also use this for

02:20:23.832 --> 02:20:26.072
feature extraction. So you can say, well, after I've trained this

02:20:26.072 --> 02:20:28.161
thing, maybe I'm gonna use for model free RL.

02:20:28.352 --> 02:20:30.672
It turns out that you can prove under very

02:20:30.992 --> 02:20:33.111
fairly strong assumptions that phi of

02:20:33.111 --> 02:20:35.302
s, the representation of current states, actually

02:20:35.361 --> 02:20:37.611
sufficient to represent any queue function

02:20:37.611 --> 02:20:39.652
for any reward. Long as you've trained everything to

02:20:39.652 --> 02:20:41.992
convergence and the limit of infinitely many samples.

02:20:42.222 --> 02:20:44.352
So that's basically the same as just using five

02:20:44.491 --> 02:20:46.572
s in encoder of states putting a linear head

02:20:46.572 --> 02:20:48.252
on top of it for your Q function.

02:20:48.812 --> 02:20:50.932
And the theory shows that this is sufficient in

02:20:50.932 --> 02:20:52.952
the limit of everything being, you know, infinitely

02:20:53.013 --> 02:20:55.103
asymptotic. And

02:20:55.103 --> 02:20:57.512
this thing actually works. You can use it for

02:20:58.232 --> 02:21:00.651
some RL benchmark tasks like navigating a maze,

02:21:00.852 --> 02:21:03.042
also use it for learning Atari games

02:21:03.042 --> 02:21:04.582
from images. We actually

02:21:05.292 --> 02:21:07.612
had a funny set of experiments where

02:21:07.612 --> 02:21:09.742
we harvested Atari videos from

02:21:09.742 --> 02:21:11.751
YouTube so they're for

02:21:11.751 --> 02:21:13.991
some reason people post YouTube videos of themselves playing

02:21:13.991 --> 02:21:16.013
Atari games, you can use it to train one of these

02:21:16.013 --> 02:21:18.172
intention condition value functions that doesn't actually

02:21:18.172 --> 02:21:20.512
require actions during training, and then you

02:21:20.512 --> 02:21:22.672
can use it, as a feature representation for

02:21:22.672 --> 02:21:24.822
various rapid. Queue learning to learn each

02:21:24.822 --> 02:21:25.832
of the Atari games.

02:21:27.432 --> 02:21:29.502
So Okay. And then to kind of

02:21:29.502 --> 02:21:31.582
bring this back to the modern age, one of the things I

02:21:31.582 --> 02:21:33.792
want to mention is a very recent

02:21:33.792 --> 02:21:35.871
paper that combines these ideas, that combines

02:21:35.871 --> 02:21:38.281
the generative TD updates, intention conditioning

02:21:39.322 --> 02:21:41.162
that was actually presented at this site later by,

02:21:42.432 --> 02:21:44.661
Zhang, Park, and Eisenbach

02:21:44.661 --> 02:21:46.872
from Princeton. Where it

02:21:46.872 --> 02:21:48.872
used essentially a pretraining recipe

02:21:49.112 --> 02:21:51.192
that puts these things together with modern flow

02:21:51.192 --> 02:21:53.532
master models So it trains an

02:21:53.532 --> 02:21:55.281
intention, condition, success of representation

02:21:55.702 --> 02:21:57.813
with flow matching and this generative TD

02:21:57.813 --> 02:22:00.012
update in a pre training

02:22:00.012 --> 02:22:02.233
phase. So starting with some offline

02:22:02.233 --> 02:22:04.312
data, it discovers a set of skills in that

02:22:04.312 --> 02:22:06.342
data. Those skills are the intentions, and that

02:22:06.342 --> 02:22:08.442
uses mutual information skill discovery

02:22:08.773 --> 02:22:11.222
then uses essentially, the gamma models

02:22:11.742 --> 02:22:13.802
generative TD backup to learn successors

02:22:13.802 --> 02:22:15.872
for each of those skills, So this

02:22:15.872 --> 02:22:18.273
is the full objective, but it's it's basically just the

02:22:18.273 --> 02:22:20.632
flow matching counterpart to the general

02:22:20.632 --> 02:22:22.732
of TD equation that I had before. So it

02:22:22.732 --> 02:22:24.672
has the same two terms, one minus

02:22:24.812 --> 02:22:26.911
gamma times the next state. Plus

02:22:26.911 --> 02:22:29.063
gamma times future states sample

02:22:29.063 --> 02:22:31.092
from your model. And then, once you've trained

02:22:31.093 --> 02:22:33.132
this thing, then you can have a post training phase where

02:22:33.132 --> 02:22:35.233
you essentially use it to bootstrap RL.

02:22:35.532 --> 02:22:37.692
So, you can get an initial queue function for your

02:22:37.692 --> 02:22:39.701
task, by selecting,

02:22:39.861 --> 02:22:42.122
by basically using your successor representation

02:22:42.122 --> 02:22:44.401
to evaluate each of your skills kind of

02:22:44.401 --> 02:22:46.642
a big equation, but it's saying something very simple, which is

02:22:46.642 --> 02:22:48.878
sample from your successor representation, for

02:22:48.878 --> 02:22:50.901
each of your skills, use it to evaluate

02:22:50.901 --> 02:22:53.102
how good that's each of those skills is for your task.

02:22:53.661 --> 02:22:55.821
And that gives you those value functions just like

02:22:55.821 --> 02:22:57.852
before. Then you can use that in a

02:22:57.853 --> 02:22:59.952
kind of a generalized policy iteration

02:23:00.172 --> 02:23:02.502
framework to then refine the best skills further.

02:23:02.722 --> 02:23:04.842
The equations are basically on implicit

02:23:05.062 --> 02:23:07.222
q learning, Roughly speaking, you can think of it as

02:23:07.622 --> 02:23:09.582
taking a max over your skills at each state.

02:23:09.742 --> 02:23:11.842
Just like treating your skills as though they

02:23:12.063 --> 02:23:14.012
were actions, maximizing over the skills to find

02:23:14.252 --> 02:23:16.272
one most likely to perform your task in each state

02:23:16.432 --> 02:23:18.473
and then iterating. And this ends

02:23:18.473 --> 02:23:20.553
up working pretty well, so it does very well

02:23:20.553 --> 02:23:22.571
on the on these, benchmark tasks that's

02:23:22.571 --> 02:23:24.282
called OGBench. This is a

02:23:24.662 --> 02:23:26.802
modern offline RL benchmark. It has

02:23:26.802 --> 02:23:28.882
these, like, fairly difficult robotic manipulation

02:23:28.883 --> 02:23:30.892
tasks like solving the the lights

02:23:30.892 --> 02:23:32.912
puzzle or putting objects away in a

02:23:32.912 --> 02:23:34.262
drawer, This method actually

02:23:35.082 --> 02:23:37.182
matches or exceeds the state of the art

02:23:37.182 --> 02:23:39.202
the time when it was published. So that's pretty

02:23:39.202 --> 02:23:41.603
neat. That means that, you can actually instantiate these things

02:23:41.603 --> 02:23:43.667
with modern generative techniques and they actually

02:23:43.667 --> 02:23:46.132
work. Okay.

02:23:46.592 --> 02:23:48.633
Now there is an important special

02:23:48.633 --> 02:23:50.952
case of this kind of framework that I want to mention, that's

02:23:50.952 --> 02:23:52.952
coming back to the title of my talk, which is that

02:23:53.192 --> 02:23:55.522
successor representations, they actually have a very close

02:23:55.576 --> 02:23:57.707
connection to goal conditioned reinforcement

02:23:57.707 --> 02:24:00.182
learning. In fact, you can think of goal conditioned reinforcement

02:24:00.402 --> 02:24:02.342
learning as a special case, as a restriction

02:24:02.842 --> 02:24:04.392
of these more general value functions,

02:24:04.872 --> 02:24:07.112
to the setting where all you care about is reaching

02:24:07.112 --> 02:24:09.291
goals. So it's a special case of intention. In general,

02:24:09.291 --> 02:24:11.312
your intention might be some arbitrary

02:24:11.451 --> 02:24:13.542
set of future states, if your intention is

02:24:13.542 --> 02:24:15.902
a single future state, then this essentially

02:24:16.143 --> 02:24:17.442
goal conditioned reinforcement learning.

02:24:18.642 --> 02:24:20.802
And goal conditioned reinforcement learning is a is an

02:24:20.802 --> 02:24:22.901
important special case because it has

02:24:22.901 --> 02:24:24.042
some special structure.

02:24:25.082 --> 02:24:27.302
Here's a special structure. If you,

02:24:28.111 --> 02:24:30.052
make a small transformation

02:24:30.781 --> 02:24:32.861
of the original framework, which has a reward

02:24:32.861 --> 02:24:34.912
of one for reaching the state and zero everywhere

02:24:34.912 --> 02:24:36.962
else, you subtract a constant, you

02:24:36.962 --> 02:24:39.217
subtract one from everything, then

02:24:39.462 --> 02:24:41.482
your value function becomes a kind of a temporal

02:24:41.542 --> 02:24:43.702
distance because you get a reward of minus one for

02:24:43.702 --> 02:24:45.762
every time step until you reach the goal,

02:24:45.762 --> 02:24:47.842
and zero when you reach it. So that

02:24:47.842 --> 02:24:49.462
means that the negative of your value

02:24:50.057 --> 02:24:51.892
is how far you are

02:24:52.132 --> 02:24:54.292
from your destination. And that means

02:24:54.292 --> 02:24:56.472
that the negative of your value function obeys

02:24:57.182 --> 02:24:59.222
certain geometric properties. It looks a lot

02:24:59.222 --> 02:25:01.382
like a distance. It's not actually a distance because a proper

02:25:01.382 --> 02:25:03.642
distance should be symmetric, but it is a quasi

02:25:03.642 --> 02:25:05.901
metric. So kind of an asymmetric distance,

02:25:05.901 --> 02:25:07.232
and it obeys a triangle and

02:25:08.672 --> 02:25:11.142
transitive property, which is say the distance

02:25:11.142 --> 02:25:12.982
for reaching some distant point can be

02:25:13.222 --> 02:25:15.302
bounded by the distance of reaching something in between, and

02:25:15.302 --> 02:25:17.353
then from there, going to your goal. This

02:25:17.353 --> 02:25:19.692
is again, this is not a new idea. This is a fairly

02:25:19.912 --> 02:25:22.002
established idea. But the geometry of this

02:25:22.002 --> 02:25:24.032
allows you to take

02:25:24.232 --> 02:25:26.331
some shortcuts get more generalizable value

02:25:26.331 --> 02:25:26.831
functions.

02:25:28.541 --> 02:25:30.621
So, one of the things you can do is you can

02:25:30.621 --> 02:25:32.562
use a neural network architecture

02:25:33.172 --> 02:25:35.352
that captures this quasimetric property.

02:25:35.361 --> 02:25:37.392
Again, this is not an idea that we came up

02:25:37.392 --> 02:25:39.442
with, you can use something called a metric residual network

02:25:39.682 --> 02:25:41.722
which basically by construction enforces

02:25:41.722 --> 02:25:43.962
triangle inequality And then you

02:25:43.962 --> 02:25:46.052
can learn a feature representation

02:25:46.052 --> 02:25:48.208
of your states and goals but now

02:25:48.208 --> 02:25:50.342
this feature representation is going to obey

02:25:50.342 --> 02:25:52.371
that geometric property. So, if you

02:25:52.371 --> 02:25:54.792
want a little bit of intuition for it, it's a little bit like embedding

02:25:54.832 --> 02:25:56.932
your states in a Euclidean space where distances

02:25:57.072 --> 02:25:59.091
are temporal distances

02:25:59.092 --> 02:26:01.202
except that it's not a Euclidean space anymore because

02:26:01.202 --> 02:26:03.352
it's a quasi metric rather than a full metric. But

02:26:03.353 --> 02:26:05.422
roughly the same intuition. And

02:26:05.422 --> 02:26:07.772
when you do this, you get better generalization

02:26:07.912 --> 02:26:10.412
because now you can construct your embedding using nearby

02:26:10.472 --> 02:26:12.627
states and have that embedding actually

02:26:12.627 --> 02:26:14.672
generalized to longer distances. And that means

02:26:14.672 --> 02:26:17.082
that you can accomplish more distant

02:26:17.082 --> 02:26:19.272
goals the ones you trained on. So the key idea

02:26:19.272 --> 02:26:21.282
that is that if trained properly, the features

02:26:21.282 --> 02:26:23.603
actually generalize. And you can show

02:26:23.603 --> 02:26:25.522
this theoretically as well. So

02:26:25.942 --> 02:26:28.023
So, here's a little experiment on

02:26:28.023 --> 02:26:30.192
this. A real world robotic scenario.

02:26:30.422 --> 02:26:32.882
Took the bridge dataset, which consists of relatively

02:26:32.882 --> 02:26:34.942
short atomic skills, like picking up one

02:26:34.942 --> 02:26:37.332
object and putting it in a container. We trained

02:26:37.332 --> 02:26:39.507
one of these distance models

02:26:39.862 --> 02:26:42.023
these generalizable features. The method is called MQL.

02:26:42.023 --> 02:26:44.092
It was actually presented at ICLIA this year.

02:26:44.727 --> 02:26:47.062
And then we tested it just by commanding

02:26:47.202 --> 02:26:49.542
a more distant goal. In this case, the goal requires

02:26:49.902 --> 02:26:51.911
putting multiple objects on the plate. There

02:26:51.911 --> 02:26:53.991
is no explicit planning over these objects.

02:26:53.991 --> 02:26:56.122
It's literally just commanding a different goal

02:26:56.122 --> 02:26:58.062
to a model trained on only on goals

02:26:58.462 --> 02:27:00.912
required moving one object. So it really is kind of magical

02:27:00.912 --> 02:27:02.942
generalization, metric

02:27:02.942 --> 02:27:04.912
property. In this case, it's four objects.

02:27:05.362 --> 02:27:07.382
And this method actually works quite well.

02:27:07.442 --> 02:27:09.513
So, we have bank

02:27:09.513 --> 02:27:11.642
of different evaluations single pick in

02:27:11.642 --> 02:27:13.102
place, double, triple, quadruple,

02:27:13.867 --> 02:27:16.012
and the more complex it gets, the more out

02:27:16.012 --> 02:27:18.112
of distribution, the larger the advantage of this

02:27:18.542 --> 02:27:20.701
metric method, m k, the one the

02:27:20.701 --> 02:27:22.042
one labeled in dark green.

02:27:23.483 --> 02:27:25.562
And it works on benchmark tasks as well, and that's

02:27:25.562 --> 02:27:26.392
that's quite nice.

02:27:27.752 --> 02:27:29.832
Okay. So, at this point, I see that I'm running a

02:27:29.832 --> 02:27:31.942
little low on time. So I'm gonna actually,

02:27:32.082 --> 02:27:34.023
skip ahead a little bit to

02:27:34.632 --> 02:27:36.872
the conclusion and then I'll take some questions. There's some

02:27:36.872 --> 02:27:38.802
other parts about using models, but

02:27:38.882 --> 02:27:40.742
and and LLMs. You can do this with LLMs

02:27:40.962 --> 02:27:43.142
as well. But in the interest of time, I'll skip through

02:27:43.142 --> 02:27:45.392
this. Just go to the takeaways.

02:27:45.611 --> 02:27:47.652
So world models

02:27:48.133 --> 02:27:50.472
don't need to predict the next observation. And I think

02:27:50.472 --> 02:27:52.632
it's actually very natural because when we think about

02:27:52.632 --> 02:27:54.782
future events we don't necessarily imagine

02:27:54.932 --> 02:27:56.972
a time synchronized movie of everything

02:27:56.972 --> 02:27:59.112
that can happen. We think about like, hey, if I do this, here

02:27:59.273 --> 02:28:01.332
an outcome that might happen at some point in the future.

02:28:01.892 --> 02:28:03.933
A good outcome or a bad outcome. This will happen at some point in

02:28:03.933 --> 02:28:06.162
the future is all you really need to plan

02:28:06.162 --> 02:28:08.342
and act. And successive representations,

02:28:08.342 --> 02:28:10.523
even though it's a very old idea, can be instantiated

02:28:10.892 --> 02:28:13.052
with modern generative models, and we can

02:28:13.052 --> 02:28:14.922
use these types of models to also predict

02:28:15.412 --> 02:28:17.082
counterfactuals conditional intentions,

02:28:17.422 --> 02:28:19.582
which allows us to essentially implement a kind

02:28:19.582 --> 02:28:21.803
of planning Only the planning is much easier now

02:28:21.803 --> 02:28:23.991
because you don't have to actually think about all the

02:28:23.991 --> 02:28:26.142
sequential actions. Just the overall outcomes.

02:28:26.302 --> 02:28:28.462
It's a special kind of a special kind of intention is

02:28:28.462 --> 02:28:30.442
a goal, and that admits some really

02:28:30.501 --> 02:28:32.512
nice structure and more

02:28:33.142 --> 02:28:35.212
effective algorithms. These things can actually work

02:28:35.212 --> 02:28:37.442
with robots in the real world. Which can

02:28:37.442 --> 02:28:39.842
make them pretty practical can also work with LLMs.

02:28:39.842 --> 02:28:42.081
I didn't get to that part, but you can train goal conditioned

02:28:42.081 --> 02:28:44.282
language models actually get them to achieve conversational

02:28:44.422 --> 02:28:46.482
goals. And that can work very well also.

